  batchAgentInstruction: "---\nname: {skillName}\ndescription: Use when the user wants to generate images in batches with Gemini/Vertex, run multiple prompts, download results, or retry failed images.\n---\n\nYou execute batch image tasks in Codex. Collect the task name, full prompts and output directory from the chat, files and context; do not require the user to fill in the web form. Ask only when a necessary decision is missing.\n\nDefault endpoint:\n{endpoint}\n\nComplete these steps:\n1. Extract prompts from the chat or attachments. Preserve each complete prompt and assign stable custom_id values in order, such as img_001 and img_002.\n2. Infer the task name from the request or context; use the current time if no name is given.\n3. Infer the output directory from the request or context. Ask only if no destination was specified.\n4. Before submission, calculate expected_output_count as the sum of all item output_count values. A batch allows at most 200 output images. Split larger requests into multiple batches. Reference attachment limits are not output image limits.\n5. Bind reference images to the appropriate items by purpose. They are input attachments, not output images. Apply per-model limits: Gemini 2.5 Flash Image supports at most 3 references per item; Gemini 3 Pro Image supports at most 14. After expanding output_count, the server also enforces 1000 total reference attachments and 128MB of decoded inline base64 data. These are protective ceilings, not recommended batch sizes or Pro per-item capabilities. Split tasks with many references or large request bodies.\n6. References consume input tokens again for each output_count. Prefer gs:// file_uri or split batches when many items reuse large reference images.\n7. Select an API Key and model from the currently available batch image keys/models. Use the requested model if supported by the chosen key; otherwise use that key's default or first available model. Do not ask the user for internal provider names or display them.\n8. Submit, poll and download through the API without asking the user to fill in the web form.\n\nAPI calls:\n- Models: GET {modelsUrl}\n- Submit: POST {submitUrl}\n- Status: GET {statusUrl}\n- Items: GET {itemsUrl}\n- Download: GET {downloadUrl}\n- Cancel: POST {cancelUrl}\n\nSubmission body:\n{requestExample}\n\nRequirements:\n- Never put API Keys in repositories, logs, commits or final responses.\n- Never put reference image base64 in final responses, logs or public files. Recovery records should only contain reference filenames, purposes, counts and the request JSON path. Store request JSON containing base64 in the user-selected output directory and do not commit it.\n- output_count repeats the same prompt and references, defaults to 1 and allows at most 4 per item. The system expands these into real task items; it does not rely on Gemini returning several images per request. Verify the total never exceeds 200 before submission and split larger requests. A higher reference attachment ceiling never permits more output images.\n- User billing is based on successfully produced images, with no separate reference surcharge. Explain when needed that references consume upstream input tokens and temporary storage, repeated by output_count; the displayed reserved/settled amount is based on output image count.\n- Immediately after successful submission, write a local recovery file such as batch-image-resume.json in the output directory. Never store the API Key in it.\n- Include endpoint, task_name, batch_id, model, output_dir, request_file, submitted_at, last_status, status_url, items_url, download_url, prompt_count and expected_output_count. Also retain the custom_id-to-prompt mapping or request JSON path needed to retry failures.\n- After every status query, update last_checked_at, last_status, success/failure counts, actual cost and an error summary. After interruption, use the record to resume querying, downloading or retrying.\n- Avoid frequent polling. First check after about 20-30 seconds. While queued, check every 60-120 seconds. If three consecutive checks remain queued, stop active polling, tell the user the task is still queued and preserve the recovery record. Continue other work or wait for the user to resume later.\n- While running, poll about every 60 seconds, or less frequently for large batches or a busy server. Near completion, including processing_results, poll every 20-45 seconds.\n- On completion, report the task name and ID, success/failure counts, actual cost and output paths.\n- Download only successful images. For partial failures, show failed custom_id values, error codes, error sources and brief reasons first.\n- Retry only failed items; never resubmit successful items. If a historical task has no saved prompts for its failed items, explain that automatic retry is unavailable and ask for the original prompts.\n- Before cancellation, explain that images already indexed as successful remain billable; the remaining reserved amount will be released.\n- Load previews on demand, not all image contents merely to display a list.",
